#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true
#
# tools/core/compare-lowering -- decision 0026 steps 4 and 5: the old and the
# new lowering tools, run side by side over the route suites.
#
#   compare-lowering freeze --tree TREE --to DIR
#   ALLOW_ORACLE=1 compare-lowering run --old DIR --new DIR --work DIR \
#       [--route NAME]... [--perturb object|mlir|text] [--counts FILE|none]
#   compare-lowering report --work DIR [--counts FILE|none]
#
# Decision 0026's Validation: "compare the old and the new tool's output: the
# generic-printed MLIR with implicit file locations stripped and explicit Ruby
# locations kept, and the object bytes. Both arms must exit 0 with non-empty
# output, and a 1-byte test-only change must be refused by name. Refusal
# diagnostics are compared as full text." The owner authorized that before/after
# comparison for 0026 steps 4 and 5 only (2026-10-23); it is gated as an oracle
# (tests/ORACLES.tsv) so that it runs only when asked.
#
# FREEZE copies a tree's built tools into DIR in the tree's own layout:
# tools/mlir/native/build/bin/* and tools/contract/build/spinel-typedump with
# its stamp. DIR/SHA256SUMS lists every copied file; `run` refuses an arm whose
# files no longer match it.
#
# THE ARMS. The tools find files by walking up from their own executable: the
# ledger (tools/platform/menus.yaml), the profiles it names, the int64 table
# (tools/core/int64-allow.tsv, whose rows name sources from the root it is
# found under), the reader and spinel-fs-codegen. So `run` makes each arm a
# tree at WORK/arms/ARM: its frozen files copied in (and checked against
# SHA256SUMS again), everything else a link into this checkout. Both arms find
# the same ledger, profiles and table, this checkout's; only the binaries
# differ.
#
# RUN drives the route suites with a build directory, WORK/build, whose
# spinel-core-kernel, spinel-core-emit, spinel-fs-emit, spinel-fs-grad,
# spinel-core-boundary, spinel-core-adapter, spinel-core-package,
# spinel-core-check and spinel-core-stack are wrappers (TOOLS; every one links
# a library steps 4 and 5 change); every other tool there is a link to the
# new arm's, and the new
# arm's source stamp sits beside them, so a suite's stamp check sees the new
# tool exactly as it would see the real one. Each wrapper call runs the old
# and the new tool on the same arguments, each arm with its output files sent
# to its own directory (same base names), and compares:
#
#   * the exit status, and stdout and stderr as full text;
#   * every output file the arguments name, byte for byte;
#   * the MLIR: the call's own --emit-mlir, or one this adds where the tool
#     takes it (spinel-core-kernel, spinel-core-emit, spinel-fs-emit outside
#     its product and probe forms) as a side output. Each is re-printed by
#     mlir-opt in generic form with locations, and the locations mlir-opt gave
#     from the file it parsed (the implicit ones) are removed; a location the
#     tool wrote (`loc("x.rb":L:C)`) is kept.
#
# In text, each arm's own output directory is read as the path the call named,
# each arm's install root as <TOOLS> and the digest of its own tool as
# <TOOL-SHA256> (the kernel's report names its executable and records its
# digest). Then the new arm's outputs and status are handed to the suite,
# which goes on as with the new tool. ALLOW_ORACLE is removed before any suite
# runs: the suites' own oracle sections stay gated.
#
# One row per call in WORK/calls.tsv: route, tool, verdict, the two statuses,
# the call's directory under WORK/calls. A verdict is `same` (both exit 0,
# every named output non-empty), `same-refusal` (both refuse, the same text),
# `DIFF <what>` or `EMPTY <what>`.
#
# A RUN THAT DOES NOT COMPARE EVERY CALL MUST NOT SAY "no difference". Two
# checks see to it:
#
#   * the counts. The report counts calls per route and tool (WORK/counts.tsv)
#     and checks them against tests/compare-lowering/counts.tsv, the tracked
#     baseline, or against --counts FILE; a count that differs fails by route
#     and tool (`COUNT`). Only the routes the run ran are checked. `--counts
#     none` writes the counts without checking them, to make a new baseline:
#     its report says NOT CHECKED.
#   * the route logs. A lane that could not run, a skip, or a tool built from
#     other source than the tree's fails by route and lane (`NOT-RUN`,
#     `STALE`), unless the suite declares it: a gated oracle section that
#     tests/ORACLES.tsv lists, or a skip tests/compare-lowering/skips.tsv
#     names by route, lane and reason. A summary that counts more skips or
#     lanes that could not run than the log names fails too. The declared
#     skips are listed by name in the report.
#
# Exits 1 on any DIFF or EMPTY row, COUNT, NOT-RUN or STALE, or a route that
# called none of the wrapped tools.
#
# WHAT THE ROUTES READ FROM A TREE BUILD. Each path a route suite resolves
# under tools/.../build or vendor/, and whether the comparison covers it:
#
#   wrapped (WORK/build/bin, through SPINEL_CORE_BUILD and the variable named):
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-core-kernel     KERNEL
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-core-emit       SPINEL_CORE_BUILD
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-fs-emit         SPINEL_CORE_BUILD
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-fs-grad         SPINEL_CORE_BUILD
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-core-boundary   BOUNDARY
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-core-adapter    ADAPTER
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-core-package    PACKAGE
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-core-check      CORE_CHECK, SPINEL_CORE_BUILD
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-core-stack      STACK_TOOL
#   the new arm's, not compared:
#     tools/mlir/native/build/bin/spinel-fs-check, and spinel-fs-codegen
#       (found by spinel-fs-emit beside itself), spinel-rtcheck: object gates
#       and code generation that link none of SpinelMLIRScalar,
#       SpinelCoreEligibility or SpinelCoreKernel (spinel-fs-check takes two
#       inline AST readers from lib/Frontend/Scalar/ScalarAst.h). None of
#       their sources changed from 8c38624 to step 5, and the three binaries
#       are byte for byte the same in the frozen c7affd5 and step 5 arms.
#     tools/contract/build/spinel-typedump (SPINEL_TYPEDUMP, or found from the
#       tool's own directory): the reader, which no step of 0026 changes.
#   the tree's own, not compared: none of them is lowering code.
#     tools/contract/build/spinel-typedump where tests/freestanding/stamps.sh
#       and lane-env.sh name it with no override (the same reader, held to the
#       tree by its stamp)
#     tools/llvm/build/libspinel_rt_lto.a (BC_DIR), the runtime bitcode
#     tools/llvm/passes/build/libSpinelGCRoots.so, the GC-roots pass
#     tools/grad/spinel-gradgen and Enzyme (ENZYME), the gradient generator
#     vendor/spinel/bin/spinel (SPINEL_SEAM, SPINEL), the pinned compiler
#
# THE TREE MUST BE THE NEW ARM'S SOURCE. The suites check the source stamp of
# the tools they resolve, and the wrappers present the new arm's; an identity
# pass (old and new both copies of one build) therefore runs with the tree at
# that build's source, or the suites refuse before calling anything (the
# report then shows routes with no calls).
#
# --perturb is the control that a difference is found and named: it changes
# one byte of the new arm's object (object), of its MLIR (mlir), or adds one
# to its stderr (text), after the new arm ran and before the comparison.
#
# Routes (--route, default all): native-core-driver, native-core-ledger,
# native-core-run, native-core-arith, core-boundary, doom-control-flow,
# doom-borrowed-calls, doom-entries (the three entries packed and built for
# this host and for the Cortex-M7, as rt1186.sh builds them), app-cross,
# app-lowering, lowering (tests/lowering), freestanding (tests/freestanding/
# run.sh, with FS_WORK under WORK). The suites take LLVM_PREFIX and the lane
# tools (ENZYME, ZEPHYR_BASE, ...) from the environment, as each one's header
# says.

